33
00:06:02.070 --> 00:06:03.920
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Sure, yeah, thank you, Tracy.

34
00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:08.580
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Yeah, I see you've got it, yeah, pulled up there.

35
00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:17.799
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So yeah, the PR has been open, probably for nearly a week, I think.

36
00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:36.900
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And, several people from Kaleido, particularly from… who have worked on, or, or, or, been instrumental in the development of Paladin and Zeto have joined the course, so we have Peter Broadhurst, some of you may be familiar with Peter from his involvement with Firefly.

37
00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:52.380
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And one of the founders of Kaleido, Jim Zhang, similarly on both counts, and has been heavily involved in the Zeto side of the Paladin project, and Andrew Richardson, who has been doing a lot of work on both Firefly and Paladin over, over, the last couple of years.

38
00:06:53.150 --> 00:07:08.309
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And just to give a… I guess… I guess, I'm looking for you, Tracy, for a little bit of guidance as to how to kind of go through the proposal, but I was going to suggest maybe I give a quick 5-minute recap of Paladin and why we're bringing this proposal forward.

39
00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:18.530
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Maybe perhaps open it to, to some on the Paladin Collado side who may want to chip in with, with some comments and, and, and,

40
00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.370
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): provide some more detail on areas, and then perhaps open it for questions. Is that a good way to go about it, Tracy?

41
00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:25.749
Tracy Kuhrt: Sounds like a great, approach.

42
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:35.119
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Great, thank you. And Peter, Andrew, Jim, Enrique, do feel free to chip in as I go through if you think that there are things that I've not covered sufficiently.

43
00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:41.869
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So, to give a very high-level overview for those who aren't familiar with Paladin.

44
00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:52.159
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Paladin provides, a programmable privacy framework, for EVM blockchains.

45
00:07:52.720 --> 00:08:05.030
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And it provides, out of the box, both, two, predefined token formats, which we can talk about more in a moment, notarized tokens and zero-knowledge tokens.

46
00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:11.430
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): and a programmable privacy domain, which is, called Pente.

47
00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:14.070
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And…

48
00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:25.759
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): the intent of Paladin is to solve the problem of how you achieve privacy on both permissioned and public blockchains.

49
00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:43.329
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): more specifically, how you… how you can deploy tokens and, have programmability of those tokens, do atomic swaps, DVP-like, processes, with the right level of privacy for your business, environment.

50
00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:54.049
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Paladin, for a bit more background on where Paladin sits in the LFDT currently, Paladin is, a… an LFDT lab.

51
00:08:54.390 --> 00:09:07.999
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And this proposal is actually referring to two labs, the Paladin lab, as it currently stands, and also the Zeto lab, which was created before the switch over to LFDT, so that's a Hyperledger lab.

52
00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:16.949
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And this proposal, includes bringing those two labs together under one LFDT project banner,

53
00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.680
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Albeit keeping their repositories, their codebases separated.

54
00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:33.279
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And the intent is really to take Paladin to the next step along its journey to, maturity, to awareness.

55
00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.739
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): To, a broader audience.

56
00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:54.200
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): to a… hopefully a growing num… Kind of community of maintainers and developers, and as others, I'm sure we'll talk about when we get into the detail, Paladin is very pluggable, and some of the concepts that are described here about, zero-knowledge tokens and no-trice tokens, and EVM smart contracts.

57
00:09:54.200 --> 00:10:05.340
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): that are within a privacy group, those are all pluggable, and we encourage people to look at Paladin as a framework into which they can add their own privacy mechanisms.

58
00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:10.690
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): It's probably worth…

59
00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:33.680
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): kind of covering from a… I guess, from some of the… some of the aspects of the proposal template for LFDT that pretty much everyone I've named so far has been heavily active in a number of LFDT projects, so they're very familiar with both open source development and the LFDT slash Hyperledger framework, that, those other projects fall under.

60
00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:42.930
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Our… our intent is that Paladin will… will have its first major release in the very near future.

61
00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:51.219
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And… This proposal somewhat aligns with that, with that process.

62
00:10:54.600 --> 00:11:07.869
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Now, I don't… I'm just going to pause there for a second. I've covered a little bit of that context that Tracy's showing about the two independent labs at the moment, and the fact that this is intended to bring them together.

63
00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:15.570
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): I don't… if you want to scroll down a little further through the proposal, Tracy, maybe I'll just stop you as we go,

64
00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:34.630
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): there's a bit about… in here about just the fundamental need in the EVM space for privacy. That's a kind of an absolute given now in the blockchain space, and particularly in the EVM space. And just to kind of give a very high level of the solution.

65
00:11:34.770 --> 00:11:41.090
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Paladin… acts as, I guess,

66
00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:48.450
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): An off-chain management, transaction management component to, a base ledger of some sort?

67
00:11:49.280 --> 00:12:00.430
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): where an example-based ledger might be a permission Besu chain, or another really good example might be a level 1 chain like Ethereum, or a level 2 chain like Linea.

68
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:10.980
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): It, it supports, it fully supports EVM, compatible, smart contracts being deployed into privacy groups.

69
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:30.870
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And as I mentioned at the beginning, it supports deploying out of the box, using its predefined token formats, both notarized tokens, where you have a notary as a party who is party to all transactions, and potentially involved in all transactions, and has visibility of them.

70
00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:41.649
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): But also a zero-knowledge-based token with varying degrees of anonymity and confidentiality based on the needs you have, the gas costs you're willing to consume, and so on.

71
00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:52.169
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And if people have questions specifically about the Zeto and the zero-knowledge token side of Paladin, as I said, Jim Zhang is on the call and can go into much more detail about that.

72
00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:58.969
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Interestingly, Paladin actually brings in, part of Bessu.

73
00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:03.070
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): in its actual runtime. So,

74
00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:10.789
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): if you keep scrolling down a little, I think the effort and resources section just talks a little bit about some of the technologies,

75
00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:17.099
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So, Paldin is primarily written in Golang, but as I said, it pulls in,

76
00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:31.019
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): substantial, libraries from BESU in order to provide, EVM, smart contract execution, and also EVM state management.

77
00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:49.040
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So there is a reasonable amount of Java. The privacy group domain, as we call it, called Pente, is written in Java, and that integrates with BESU. So Besu, as a robust, tried and tested technology for EVM execution and state.

78
00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:59.489
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): We actually reused that existing FDT project inside Paladin in order to run smart contracts and maintain state of smart contracts.

79
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:03.850
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So it's quite a complex project,

80
00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:17.699
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): there are some quite complex concepts involved in Paladin, and as I said, others on the call, I'm sure, can go into detail on those if people would like to kind of explore some of those further.

81
00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:23.740
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): I guess one more minute, and then I think I'll probably stop talking.

82
00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:35.249
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): we've been working towards Paladin having quite a… quite a good getting started experience already, so, both the GitHub repo and the Paladin documentation

83
00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:44.349
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Has, really clear getting started guides for developers, getting started tutorials for users.

84
00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:54.090
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And our documentation maturity is an area we've been focusing on recently as we move towards a V1 release.

85
00:14:54.180 --> 00:15:13.040
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So, Paladin's in a pretty good state now for other maintainers, other contributors to come to the project and start contributing to it. And from a user point of view, some coming to Paladin to use it and understand what it offers, understand its architecture and where it fits in the rest of the EVM landscape.

86
00:15:13.090 --> 00:15:17.070
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): The Paladin docs that I've included some links to.

87
00:15:17.360 --> 00:15:27.539
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): are pretty mature now, and they give a really good overview of a lot of those areas that people would expect of an incubating LFDT project.

88
00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:52.469
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So I'm gonna… I'm gonna pause there. I didn't want to go too far down any one avenue. I think maybe just before we invite questions, I… I'll just invite anyone from the Paladin or Zeto, development side, so Peter, Andrew, Jim, Enrique, is there anything you feel would be really useful to highlight that I've kind of perhaps skimmed over at all?

89
00:15:52.470 --> 00:16:07.969
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Yeah, and I'd like to stay up… this is Peter here. I'd like to stay just at the high level for the moment. So, why the Linux Foundation? Why, why, why does this project fit? And there's a few things I just want to double-click on from what… what Matt said.

90
00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:17.099
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So, the first is really, the enterprise needs for privacy. So, there are…

91
00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:38.330
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): the use of EVM, as a programming model for truly shared ledgers is something that's got this great traction, this great momentum in the world. And that's fantastic. But we found, and this actually came a couple of years ago now from a Linux Foundation event for BESU,

92
00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:49.679
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): But there's this real challenge for these projects to get past, the experimentation space into real, genuine production, thinking about…

93
00:16:49.770 --> 00:17:07.440
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Currencies of the whole of the country, ecosystems for, fully regulated, securities in, like, within a regulatory jurisdiction. To really get those to production, you can't skim over, the requirement for, for privacy.

94
00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:18.150
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And the needs, from an enterprise perspective, are quite different from the needs from a pureist, Web3 perspective.

95
00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:28.280
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And there wasn't really a project out there at the time that Paladin started that was tackling that head-on, that was really thinking about the enterprise needs first.

96
00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:41.540
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Now, what's really interesting about Paladin is it did not start by thinking about the problem as what we really need is a super clever piece of cryptography which is going to solve… solve privacy.

97
00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:54.440
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): it explicitly did not take that approach. There was lots of clever, cryptography approaches, like zero-knowledge proofs and stuff inside of there, but that is not what you should think of like the Paladin project.

98
00:17:54.440 --> 00:18:03.330
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Instead, what it said was, enterprises, just like with completely open tokens, like ERC20s.

99
00:18:03.330 --> 00:18:15.349
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Where they can use their keys, they know how to connect, they know how to integrate, and then users do as well with MetaMask and the like. This world of privacy-enabled smart contracts

100
00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:34.519
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): It's not only hard to build, but my goodness me, how on earth do you use it? How do you connect to it? How do you integrate with it? How do you write your own smart contracts? How do you create ecosystems where one company produces the token, another company produces the assets.

101
00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:39.790
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And then a third company maybe is creating the market-making in the middle.

102
00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:53.210
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Well, if you kind of have to build everything inside of one super special, interesting cryptography world, then it's not an ecosystem. It's one organization that's built one clever piece of cryptography.

103
00:18:53.210 --> 00:19:00.079
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): and everyone just has to fit inside of it. So Paladin, while it comes with these great reference implementations.

104
00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:24.650
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): The belief of the Paladin community is that they're actually leading class in their own rights. That's not what Paladin is about. Paladin's about proving that those, and the things that are going to supersede them in a year's time, the new cryptography that's going to come along, the new engines, the upgraded smart contract engines, etc, all of these that come along.

105
00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:49.609
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): you can build solutions now that are future-proof, because that's the point of EVM. And it's about bringing that promise, this minimum viable ecosystem promise that's been so amazing for public Web3, bringing that to enterprise in a truly open and modular way. So I said, I worked for Waffle for quite a long time, that I'd, I'd, like, bring it back to why

106
00:19:49.610 --> 00:20:05.380
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Foundation, and that's because it really is about an open approach to this, which is about if somebody's got a token that they want to bring to Paladin, there's a way to plug it in. It's, if you want to build an ecosystem that's open.

107
00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:07.810
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): It's about being able to do that.

108
00:20:07.840 --> 00:20:29.550
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): in an enterprise context, and build a truly open ecosystem. And we think that that's one of the reasons why it's had so much engagement from so many organizations, where… I mean, we're talking probably over 50 large financial institutions that are… that are engaged actively with it right now, because there is this real… real need, to… to sort of…

109
00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:37.410
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Get past this barrier of adoption that exists in enterprise blockchain if we don't solve the privacy problem.

110
00:20:39.290 --> 00:20:49.600
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Hey, Jim here. I'd like to jump in with a few more comments on Zito, but before I do that, I see Tracy's hand is up. Tracy, did you want to go first?

111
00:20:50.100 --> 00:21:01.079
Tracy Kuhrt: No, if there's more to add, please add it, and then I'll ask my questions, because you may actually answer one of the questions that I have, so… I'll let you guys do your thing first, and then I'll ask my question.

112
00:21:01.300 --> 00:21:02.380
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Okay, thanks, Jason.

113
00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:15.420
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Yeah, if you scroll all the way back up to the beginning of the document, I'd like to call out some of the, contributions from research… from a very talented research team from

114
00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:30.670
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): UC Berkeley and UNC Chapel Hill. So Zeto, some of you may have seen the code, but you may not realize that Zeto as a domain implementation for Paladin is not just one token.

115
00:21:30.670 --> 00:21:39.330
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): It's a collection of, I think, at the current count, 11 tokens that, depends on your specific policy needs.

116
00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:55.979
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): within the zero-knowledge proof, what kind of policy do you need to prove? You pick your token so that you don't have to bear the burden of doing everything in heavy cryptography if you didn't need that kind of additional policies, so…

117
00:21:55.980 --> 00:22:13.539
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): One of the implementations, I think, is likely the first in the world where post-quantum encryption was performed inside a zero-knowledge-proofed circuit, so that the ciphertext that results from that is guaranteed to be

118
00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:28.420
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): decipherable by the, by the receiver, in this case, a auditor. So that was a, collaboration with, this research team in, Berkeley and, UNC. In fact, Hart himself.

119
00:22:28.420 --> 00:22:36.680
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): In his pers- you know, personal time when he's not traveling, was also involved in this effort. So just want to call this out.

120
00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:41.229
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And, and, very exciting to, to hear about your thoughts.

121
00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:51.510
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So, Tracy, I think that's the team's… she had lots of information there, so I'd love to move to the questions.

122
00:22:51.510 --> 00:23:03.529
Tracy Kuhrt: Okay, sounds great. So I actually have 3 questions. We'll take them one by one, but the first question I have is around, are there currently any adopters for Paladin and Zito?

123
00:23:03.530 --> 00:23:11.740
Tracy Kuhrt: That are using the projects as they exist today? Or, is that one of the things that is hoped to be…

124
00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:17.719
Tracy Kuhrt: Kind of… Gotten, if you will, from moving to an incubation-type stage.

125
00:23:18.410 --> 00:23:43.030
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Adoption is definitely actually very high on Paladin. A lot of independent adoption, not even just, that Kaleido as, sort of instigators of the project, are directly involved in. There are very large organizations, I'm afraid, I'm not sure I'm actually able to mention them by name, but if you, if you were to look at recent announcements, in the industry,

126
00:23:43.030 --> 00:23:46.090
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): and when people are talking about EVM and privacy together.

127
00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:57.169
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): Talking about that in the context of public chains, for example, or large financial institutions, like, thinking about how to work with public chains, large international institutions.

128
00:23:57.170 --> 00:24:07.790
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): working together to build new forms of currency, you'll be very likely to find somewhere in that mix that Paladin is involved.

129
00:24:07.790 --> 00:24:20.650
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So, so I, I, you know, it wasn't, it, it was, like, I… I personally have been directly working with,

130
00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:39.110
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): dozens of financial institutions which are, are engaging directly with Paladin. So, there was a lot of traction on that side, and that's not actually really the reason for the incubation push. Actually, the reason is that these are not toy projects.

131
00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:51.639
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): These are projects that want to get aggressively to production, real, genuine production. And, actually, that means that

