00:44:24.680 --> 00:44:38.269
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): Yeah, so I guess, I don't know if you have visibility of the chat. Peter, I really had a question about, key exchange between Paladin notes. I didn't know if that was something you wanted to touch on briefly, connectivity and key information.

248
00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:48.900
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): It's… I'd love to, but you're gonna have to stop me keeping on going to… getting too excited and talking too much about… falling too deeply into the well. So I think the short answer is, Paladin…

249
00:44:48.910 --> 00:45:06.279
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): has two kinds of keys. This is worth noting. Pretty much any good privacy technology has to deal with this, this problem. It was true for Tesla before, and Constellation before that as well. One type of key is, like, blockchain signing keys. How do I sign transactions?

250
00:45:06.310 --> 00:45:25.400
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And, Paladin has very pluggable cryptography, there. You can use lots of different, types of keys if you want to, over time. and it also has really advanced key management built in, pretty enterprise-grade key management and pluggable key management built in, out of the box. It's quite,

251
00:45:25.400 --> 00:45:29.450
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): quite an advanced feature inside of there. So that's blockchain sign-in keys.

252
00:45:29.450 --> 00:45:33.930
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And your key stays private to you, in that case.

253
00:45:34.250 --> 00:45:44.959
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So I'll come to answering the question in a second, so I want to describe that type of key, and the second type of key, which is important to understand, is different, and that's data transmission keys.

254
00:45:44.960 --> 00:46:09.910
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So, if we have private data now, it's not just being exchanged, everybody on the blockchain is receiving a copy of my data, only certain parties are going to receive a copy of it, I need to know how to transfer data privately to that party. If I'm not going to encrypt it and store it on-chain, because I'm worried about post-quantum cryptography or just a massive load on the chain that all that data will cause, I'm going to send it privately to another party.

255
00:46:09.910 --> 00:46:18.099
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): I need to know how to encrypt and transfer it off-chain to that party, so I need the transfer keys for them.

256
00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:34.239
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): So… so I've got those two different types of keys. Now, in addition to just managing those keys in the runtime, Paladin provides what's called a registry, and this can be an on-chain registry to, to actually, have a smart contract.

257
00:46:34.240 --> 00:46:52.640
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): where you say, hey, look, I want to join a private ecosystem with you, I'm going to advertise the public key credentials for transferring me data off-chain, and I'm going to push it onto the blockchain. So you have a blockchain-backed registry, and all of the smart contracts, as a starter are provided

258
00:46:52.640 --> 00:47:05.780
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): for you in the project for this, it just works out the box, but you can check… you can swap in your own contract for this, if you want to manage your ecosystem your own way, to be like, here's a trusted repository of node endpoints.

259
00:47:06.020 --> 00:47:14.130
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And everyone just uses a blockchain transaction to advertise and rotate their off-chain transmission keys.

260
00:47:14.260 --> 00:47:18.510
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): And once you've done that, if

261
00:47:18.720 --> 00:47:33.080
Peter Broadhurst (Kaleido): during creation of a transaction, I need to ask some people for signatures from them that are on-chain signing signatures. I know I'm asking the right person, because I know through this registry.

262
00:47:33.290 --> 00:47:49.179
